{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>It has stood in its place.</b> Meaning, 'in its place' — according to what the priest's eye sees, it has stood and not spread. But according to Rashi, who explained that it refers to the affliction, it should have written עומד בעיניו. Black hair is another sign of purity. And one may ask: if this one comes without that one, what is the law? And the Rav Bekhor Shor says that this depends on the dispute between Rabbi Natan and Rabbi Yishaya: according to the one who says [the verse] implies this one without that one, and implies both together — it requires two signs; and according to the one who says [the verse] implies each one independently — one sign suffices."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:08:01.880568140Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}